    "evidence_text": "Histones are incorporated in trans during reassembly of the yeast PHO5 promoter.\n\nWe have analyzed where the histones for reassembly of the inactive promoter chromatin come from.\n\nEpigenetic switch involved in activation of pioneer factor FOXA1-dependent enhancers.\n\nConcomitantly, histone H3 lysine 4 methylation is induced at these enhancers.\n\nHowever, their activities were inhibited by DNA methylation.\n\nDNA methylation status predicts cell type-specific enhancer activity.\n\nVitamin C enhances the reprogramming process, but the underlying mechanisms are unclear.\n\nHowever, the recent discovery of histone demethylases has challenged the stable nature of histone methylation.\n\nHistone Methylation-Dependent Mechanisms Impose Ligand Dependency for Gene Activation by Nuclear Receptors\n\nActivator Control of Nucleosome Occupancy in Activation and Repression of Transcription\n\nThe reverse process, repression of transcription, has often been correlated with the presence of nucleosomes.\n\nBut it is not known whether nucleosomes are required for that effect.\n\nThese results show how an activator's recruiting function can control chromatin structure both during gene activation and repression.\n\nThese findings were made possible by our nucleosome occupancy assay.\n\nControlling nuclear receptors: the circular logic of cofactor cycles",
